WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:09.480
Functions serve two purposes in Python. They&nbsp;
return a value, or they do not. In Python,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:15.200
functions can return a value, perform some action,&nbsp;
or both. The value returned by a function can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:20.760
stored in a variable for future use or for&nbsp;
further calculations. In this topic, we will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:28.400
see when the return keyword should be used and&nbsp;
when it is not needed. In this 423-return file,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:35.600
I have two functions: one that calculates a total&nbsp;
score and another that displays a welcome message&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:43.360
whenever the function is called. Without going&nbsp;
through the code yet, which of these two functions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:51.760
I just described would warrant a value stored for&nbsp;
later use? If you said the total score function,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:57.720
you would be correct. Functions that print&nbsp;
messages do not need to return any values.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:02.480
Those messages can be stored in a variable.&nbsp;
Functions that do not return any values are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:10.240
often called void functions or procedures. The&nbsp;
welcome function on lines 4 and 5 is an example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:16.040
of a function that outputs text. The reason for&nbsp;
the function is so that we can call the function,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:24.120
as we do on line 10, as many times as we want, and&nbsp;
if someone wants to change the welcome message,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:32.080
they need only do it once. Let’s go back to the&nbsp;
function on lines 1 and 2. This function has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:37.680
return statement to return the score times&nbsp;
the multiplier. Why the need for a return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:44.200
statement and not just a print statement?&nbsp;
If we print the result here, we cannot use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:55.280
it again in code. Look at the code on lines 7&nbsp;
and 8. The total score function is run twice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.280 --> 00:02:03.960
and the results are stored in two variables:&nbsp;
score1 and score2. Then, both scores are revealed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:14.680
in an f-string statement on line 11. For lines&nbsp;
7, 8, and 11 to work, the function on line 1 must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:26.160
return a value. I will run this code, and we will&nbsp;
get the welcome message and the print statement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:33.440
with the two scores. The return statement is&nbsp;
very important to understand because it allows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:40.000
a function result to be used later in code as&nbsp;
part of a print statement or further calculation.

